1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,280
Some men walk toward the storm that everyone else

2
00:00:03,280 --> 00:00:04,200
runs from.

3
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:05,660
We call them fearless.

4
00:00:05,660 --> 00:00:09,080
But what is really happening inside a fearless mind?

5
00:00:09,080 --> 00:00:10,457
Science has a surprising answer.

6
00:00:11,533 --> 00:00:15,073
To understand fearlessness, we first have to understand fear,

7
00:00:15,073 --> 00:00:17,333
and the small part of the brain that sets

8
00:00:17,333 --> 00:00:18,413
it off.

9
00:00:18,413 --> 00:00:20,933
Because fearlessness turns out to come in very different

10
00:00:20,933 --> 00:00:22,013
forms.

11
00:00:22,013 --> 00:00:24,793
Some are a gift, some are trained, and some

12
00:00:24,793 --> 00:00:25,528
are a warning.

13
00:00:26,600 --> 00:00:30,240
For most of human history, fear kept us alive.

14
00:00:30,240 --> 00:00:32,320
A rustle in the dark, a drop at the

15
00:00:32,320 --> 00:00:33,380
cliff edge.

16
00:00:33,380 --> 00:00:35,540
The people who flinched lived long enough to become

17
00:00:35,540 --> 00:00:36,520
our ancestors.

18
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:39,960
Deep in each side of the brain sits an

19
00:00:39,960 --> 00:00:43,080
almond shaped structure called the amygdala.

20
00:00:43,080 --> 00:00:45,560
Scientists call it a pivotal trigger for the feeling

21
00:00:45,560 --> 00:00:46,155
of fear.

22
00:00:47,233 --> 00:00:48,433
So what happens when it is gone?

23
00:00:48,433 --> 00:00:51,173
A woman known as S M has a rare

24
00:00:51,173 --> 00:00:55,013
disease, Urbach Wiethe, that destroyed her amygdala on both

25
00:00:55,013 --> 00:00:56,233
sides.

26
00:00:56,233 --> 00:00:59,233
In twenty eleven, a team led by Justin Feinstein

27
00:00:59,233 --> 00:01:01,569
published how she responds to danger.

28
00:01:02,633 --> 00:01:04,953
Researchers took her to a pet store.

29
00:01:04,953 --> 00:01:06,993
She went straight for the snakes.

30
00:01:06,993 --> 00:01:09,853
In one of the world's scariest haunted houses, she

31
00:01:09,853 --> 00:01:12,073
laughed, and poked one of the monsters in the

32
00:01:12,073 --> 00:01:12,340
head.

33
00:01:13,400 --> 00:01:15,460
In real life, she had been threatened with a

34
00:01:15,460 --> 00:01:17,180
knife and with a gun.

35
00:01:17,180 --> 00:01:19,640
Once, after escaping a man with a knife, she

36
00:01:19,640 --> 00:01:20,800
did not run.

37
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:22,019
She calmly walked away.

38
00:01:23,100 --> 00:01:25,340
Is she the perfect fearless person?

39
00:01:25,340 --> 00:01:26,300
Not quite.

40
00:01:26,300 --> 00:01:28,560
Without the alarm, nothing warned her.

41
00:01:28,560 --> 00:01:30,740
Her fearlessness was not a strength.

42
00:01:30,740 --> 00:01:32,180
It was a missing part.

43
00:01:32,180 --> 00:01:35,460
One patient cannot prove everything, critics warned.

44
00:01:35,460 --> 00:01:37,948
But her case shows exactly what fear is for.

45
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:41,120
Now meet Alex Honnold.

46
00:01:41,120 --> 00:01:43,880
On the third of June, twenty seventeen, he climbed

47
00:01:43,880 --> 00:01:45,220
El Capitan.

48
00:01:45,220 --> 00:01:47,860
That is nearly nine hundred metres of granite, and

49
00:01:47,860 --> 00:01:49,176
he used no rope at all.

50
00:01:50,233 --> 00:01:54,133
A year earlier, neuroscientist Jane Joseph scanned his brain

51
00:01:54,133 --> 00:01:56,613
while he looked at disturbing images.

52
00:01:56,613 --> 00:01:59,273
A control climber's amygdala lit up.

53
00:01:59,273 --> 00:02:00,751
Honnold's barely responded.

54
00:02:01,833 --> 00:02:05,333
But Honnold has always rejected the word fearless.

55
00:02:05,333 --> 00:02:08,453
Joseph wondered whether his brain simply regulates fear very

56
00:02:08,453 --> 00:02:09,313
well.

57
00:02:09,313 --> 00:02:11,519
On one climb, he talked himself through it.

58
00:02:12,600 --> 00:02:15,900
Psychologist Stanley Rachman studied another kind of nerve.

59
00:02:15,900 --> 00:02:20,600
In a nineteen eighty three study, decorated bomb disposal

60
00:02:20,600 --> 00:02:23,340
experts stayed calmer and more accurate under stress than

61
00:02:23,340 --> 00:02:23,736
others.

62
00:02:24,800 --> 00:02:28,180
From work like this, Rachman drew a sharp line.

63
00:02:28,180 --> 00:02:30,640
Fearlessness is feeling no fear.

64
00:02:30,640 --> 00:02:33,420
Courage is moving forward while you still feel it.

65
00:02:33,420 --> 00:02:35,780
By that measure, the bravest person in a room

66
00:02:35,780 --> 00:02:37,237
may be the most frightened one.

67
00:02:38,300 --> 00:02:41,160
Philosophers drew the same line more than two thousand

68
00:02:41,160 --> 00:02:42,880
years earlier.

69
00:02:42,880 --> 00:02:46,240
Aristotle called courage a middle path, between cowardice and

70
00:02:46,240 --> 00:02:46,833
rashness.

71
00:02:47,900 --> 00:02:50,400
And the man who fears nothing at all?

72
00:02:50,400 --> 00:02:52,380
Aristotle had a blunt verdict.

73
00:02:52,380 --> 00:02:54,520
Such a man would be a sort of madman,

74
00:02:54,520 --> 00:02:56,860
afraid of neither earthquakes nor the waves.

75
00:02:57,933 --> 00:03:01,593
Then there is Diogenes, who owned almost nothing.

76
00:03:01,593 --> 00:03:03,953
When Alexander the Great stood over him and offered

77
00:03:03,953 --> 00:03:07,153
him any favour he liked, Diogenes gave a famous

78
00:03:07,153 --> 00:03:07,747
reply.

79
00:03:08,800 --> 00:03:11,240
A man who wants nothing cannot be threatened with

80
00:03:11,240 --> 00:03:12,240
losing it.

81
00:03:12,240 --> 00:03:14,560
That is a rarer kind of fearlessness.

82
00:03:14,560 --> 00:03:17,540
Not bravery, but freedom from wanting.

83
00:03:17,540 --> 00:03:19,520
Alexander had an empire.

84
00:03:19,520 --> 00:03:21,621
Diogenes had almost nothing to lose.

85
00:03:22,700 --> 00:03:26,220
The Roman philosopher Seneca noticed where most fear actually

86
00:03:26,220 --> 00:03:27,280
lives.

87
00:03:27,280 --> 00:03:29,240
Not out in the world, but at night, in

88
00:03:29,240 --> 00:03:31,724
our own heads, rehearsing what might go wrong.

89
00:03:32,800 --> 00:03:35,180
He wrote to his friend Lucilius that there are

90
00:03:35,180 --> 00:03:37,620
more things likely to frighten us than to crush

91
00:03:37,620 --> 00:03:38,540
us.

92
00:03:38,540 --> 00:03:40,587
Then came the line people still quote.

93
00:03:41,667 --> 00:03:44,007
Then there is the performer, the man who boasts

94
00:03:44,007 --> 00:03:46,127
that nothing scares him.

95
00:03:46,127 --> 00:03:48,787
Real fearlessness rarely needs a crowd.

96
00:03:48,787 --> 00:03:51,395
Bravado is often fear, wearing a costume.

97
00:03:52,467 --> 00:03:55,307
And some fearlessness is a warning sign.

98
00:03:55,307 --> 00:03:59,227
In nineteen fifty seven, David Lykken found that psychopaths

99
00:03:59,227 --> 00:04:00,807
reacted less to threats.

100
00:04:00,807 --> 00:04:03,127
The evidence since then is mixed.

101
00:04:03,127 --> 00:04:05,327
Fear without a conscience is not courage.

102
00:04:05,327 --> 00:04:07,144
It is danger with a calm face.

103
00:04:08,200 --> 00:04:10,060
So can fear be trained?

104
00:04:10,060 --> 00:04:12,980
Psychologists treat phobias with exposure.

105
00:04:12,980 --> 00:04:15,420
You face the fear in small steps, again and

106
00:04:15,420 --> 00:04:18,355
again, and over time the alarm grows quieter.

107
00:04:19,433 --> 00:04:21,473
That is how courage grows.

108
00:04:21,473 --> 00:04:24,193
It does not come from silencing the alarm.

109
00:04:24,193 --> 00:04:26,773
It comes from walking the path anyway, one step

110
00:04:26,773 --> 00:04:27,412
at a time.

111
00:04:28,467 --> 00:04:30,807
Mark Twain put the whole idea into a single

112
00:04:30,807 --> 00:04:32,776
line, more than a century ago.

113
00:04:33,833 --> 00:04:35,793
So the fearless man is not the one who

114
00:04:35,793 --> 00:04:36,953
feels nothing.

115
00:04:36,953 --> 00:04:39,873
That is a missing alarm, or a madman.

116
00:04:39,873 --> 00:04:42,233
He hears the alarm clearly, and walks into the

117
00:04:42,233 --> 00:04:42,921
storm anyway.

118
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:45,660
Fear is not the enemy.

119
00:04:45,660 --> 00:04:47,200
It is information.

120
00:04:47,200 --> 00:04:49,460
The fearless man listens to it, and then decides

121
00:04:49,460 --> 00:04:50,800
for himself.

122
00:04:50,800 --> 00:04:51,900
Feel the fear.

123
00:04:51,900 --> 00:04:53,451
Then take the next step anyway.
